e-media-protection walkthrough log — 2026-10-10
Worked-case lesson: no command line by design (brief §6).
The "run" is the media inventory scheme + custody procedures + sanitization
decision table + transport log authored and self-reviewed in-session against
NIST SP 800-88 Rev. 1's decision framework (Ch. 4: categorization → reuse →
sanitization decision → verification → documentation; §2.5 Clear/Purge/Destroy
definitions verified against the fetched PDF).

Scope: fictional Kestrel Components (90 staff, precision manufacturing).
Scenario: design files on workstations/file server/backup tapes + an unowned
pile of 12 retired drives (3 still holding design data).
Artifacts authored (all fictional, labeled):
  - Inventory scheme: asset tag + type/owner/classification/location columns;
    the retired pile inventoried first and assigned an owner.
  - Labeling: 3 tiers (INTERNAL green / CONFIDENTIAL yellow / RESTRICTED red)
    with the handling rule printed on the label.
  - Custody: backup tapes in locked safe + sign-out log; bonded-courier
    transport with tamper-evident bags and two-ended handoff log; USB banned
    for RESTRICTED (one line + DLP enforcement).
  - Sanitization decision table: 5 rows (HDD redeploy → Purge; HDD leaving org
    → Destroy; expired tapes → Destroy; SSD → Purge, CE only with verified
    preconditions; RESTRICTED paper → cross-cut shred) — every row with a
    verification column.
  - Transport log excerpt (tape set KT-2026-10-03, seal numbers, both-end
    signatures).

Self-review checklist (2026-10-10):
  [x] physical/operational handling view — differentiation from the data
      lifecycle stated in §1 (retention governs life; this governs the object)
  [x] Clear/Purge/Destroy definitions verified against the SP 800-88 PDF
      (§2.5: Clear = logical techniques vs simple non-invasive recovery;
      Purge = infeasible even with lab techniques; Destroy = physical)
  [x] 800-88's "disposal without sanitization only if no impact of any kind"
